.Dd June 11, 2026
.Dt CAP_CLOFORK_LIMIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_clofork_limit
.Nd lock close-on-fork for a file descriptor
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/capsicum.h
.Ft int
.Fn cap_clofork_limit "int fd" "int state"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn cap_clofork_limit
system call provides a monotonic close-on-fork guarantee for a file
descriptor that cannot be undone by the process holding it.
.Pp
This operates independently from the traditional
.Dv FD_CLOFORK
flag managed by
.Xr fcntl 2 .
The process can freely set or clear
.Dv FD_CLOFORK
via
.Dv F_SETFD
without affecting the lock.
When
.Xr fork 2
is called, the kernel skips the locked descriptor in the child's
file descriptor table if
.Em either
the
.Dv FD_CLOFORK
flag is set
.Em or
the clofork lock is
.Dv CAP_CLOFORK_LOCKED .
.Pp
The
.Fa state
argument must be one of:
.Bl -tag -width CAP_CLOFORK_UNLOCKED
.It Dv CAP_CLOFORK_UNLOCKED
No lock.
Close-on-fork is governed solely by the
.Dv FD_CLOFORK
flag.
This is the default state for newly created descriptors.
.It Dv CAP_CLOFORK_LOCKED
The descriptor will not be inherited by children of
.Xr fork 2
regardless of the
.Dv FD_CLOFORK
flag.
.El
.Pp
The lock state is monotonically increasing: once locked, it cannot be
unlocked.
Setting
.Dv CAP_CLOFORK_LOCKED
on a descriptor that is already locked is permitted and has no effect.
.Pp
The lock state is inherited across
.Xr dup 2
but not across
.Xr fork 2
.Pq since the descriptor is not inherited at all when locked .
.Pp
Combined with
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2
and
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 ,
this completes the set of monotonic descriptor confinement controls:
.Pp
.Bl -column "cap_clofork_limit" "Cannot survive fork" -offset indent -compact
.It Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 Ta Cannot be sent via SCM_RIGHTS
.It Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 Ta Cannot survive exec
.It Fn cap_clofork_limit Ta Cannot survive fork
.El
.Pp
This system call is allowed in capability mode
.Pq see Xr cap_enter 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Confine a pair channel so it cannot escape the current process:
.Bd -literal
#include <sys/capsicum.h>

/* fd is one end of a mac_capability pair or socketpair */
cap_xfer_limit(fd, CAP_XFER_NONE);
cap_cloexec_limit(fd, CAP_CLOEXEC_LOCKED);
cap_clofork_limit(fd, CAP_CLOFORK_LOCKED);

/* fd now cannot:
 *   - be sent to another process via SCM_RIGHTS
 *   - survive execve() into a new program
 *   - be inherited by fork() children
 * The process can still set/clear FD_CLOEXEC and FD_CLOFORK
 * without error, but the locks override at exec/fork time.
 */
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn cap_clofork_limit
succeeds unless:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBADF
The
.Fa fd
argument is not a valid active descriptor.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa state
argument is not a valid lock state.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The requested
.Fa state
is less restrictive than the current lock state.
The lock can only be tightened, never relaxed.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
The running kernel was compiled without
.Cd "options CAPABILITY_MODE" .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn cap_clofork_limit
system call first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
